WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.922
This example intentionally triggers a foreign-key error.

00:00:02.922 --> 00:00:06.002
The patient we are trying to delete has a related admission.

00:00:06.002 --> 00:00:11.022
The database relationship uses a restrictive delete rule, so PostgreSQL refuses to remove the patient and

00:00:11.022 --> 00:00:13.997
leave the admission pointing to a missing record.

00:00:13.997 --> 00:00:18.552
Run the example and read the error message rather than treating it as a failure of the lesson.

00:00:18.552 --> 00:00:22.897
The database is enforcing a relationship rule that was designed in Module 2.

00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:28.037
This is an important change in perspective: not every error means your database is broken.

00:00:28.037 --> 00:00:32.952
A constraint error can be evidence that the database is successfully protecting data integrity.

00:00:32.952 --> 00:00:38.597
When you see this kind of error, ask which relationship or constraint is being protected before trying to

00:00:38.597 --> 00:00:41.587
bypass it. Constraints move important rules closer to the data.

00:00:41.587 --> 00:00:48.612
NOT NULL, CHECK, UNIQUE, and foreign keys can stop invalid states before they spread into reports or

00:00:48.612 --> 00:00:49.022
applications.

00:00:49.022 --> 00:00:54.152
Application validation is useful, but database constraints provide a second line of defense shared by every

00:00:54.152 --> 00:00:54.640
client.
